e: application/json\" -d '{\"title\":\"1984\",\"author\":\"George Orwell\",\"year\":1949}'\necho \"--- create invalid\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5099/books -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"year\":2000}'\necho \"--- list\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" http://127.0.0.1:5099/books\necho \"--- list filter\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" \"http://127.0.0.1:5099/books?author=orwell\"\necho \"--- get 1\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/1\necho \"--- get 999\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/999\necho \"--- get abc\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/abc\necho \"--- put 1\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/1 -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\"year\":1966}'\necho \"--- delete 2\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" -X DELETE http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/2\necho \"--- delete 2 again\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" -X DELETE http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/2\necho \"--- patch (405)\"; curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" -X PATCH http://127.0.0.1:5099/books/1\nkill $SERVER_PID\nrm -f /tmp/smoke_books.db\n```\n\nNote: curl -s -w \"\\n%{http_code}\\n\" — in zsh, need proper quoting. Fine in a single bash script. The tool says commands run in zsh; multiline script with && or newlines? \"DO NOT use newlines to separate commands (newlines are ok in quoted strings)\" — hmm, the guidance says avoid newline-separated commands; use `;` or `&&`. I'll chain with `;` so failures don't halt. Use a single long command with `;` separators. That's long but OK. Alternatively write a small shell script file to /tmp and run it. Cleaner: write smoke script via the write tool to /tmp? The write tool writes to workspace paths — it says absolute path; /tmp allowed? \"Use /var/folders/.../opencode for temporary work outside the workspace.\" I'll write the smoke script there. Or just one big `;`-chained command. Let me do the big chained command — it's fine.\n\nActually simpler: heredoc into a file then bash it:\n\n```bash\ncat > /tmp/smoke.sh <<'EOF'\n...\nEOF\nbash /tmp/smoke.sh\n```\n\nThe tool guidance discourages `cat` heredocs for writing files (prefer Write tool). I'll use the Write tool to create the smoke script in the temp dir, then run `bash <script>`. Clean.\n\nWhere's the temp dir: `/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/opencode`. Write smoke.sh there.\n\nOK — also final check on **pytest invocation**: run `venv/bin/python -m pytest` from workspace root. pytest.ini testpaths=tests. ✓\n\nLet me also run a quick `venv/bin/python -c \"import app, db\"` sanity (import app doesn't create DB since no module-level create_app call — importing app.py just defines functions ✓).\n\nEdge — wait: `from db import Database` inside app.py — module named `db`. Any stdlib clash? No stdlib module named `db`. ✓\n\nLet me write all the files now.\n\nAlso let me reconsider test count/organization once more and finalize the test file content. I'll write ~30 focused tests. Also make sure each test uses the `client` fixture.\n\nOne more potential gotcha: pytest 9 — `client.post(..., json=...)` API unchanged. ✓ `response.get_json()` ✓.\n\nFor the direct Database unit test — import `from db import Database` in test module; pythonpath=. handles. ✓\n\nWriting files:\n\n**db.py** — final:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"SQLite data-access layer for the book collection API.\n\nThe :class:`Database` wraps a single sqlite3 connection guarded by a lock,\nwhich keeps the service thread-safe while also allowing in-memory\ndatabases (``:memory:``) to persist for the lifetime of the app.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport sqlite3\nimport threading\n\nSCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year INTEGER,\n    isbn TEXT\n);\n\"\"\"\n\n_SELECT = \"SELECT id, title, author, year, isbn FROM books\"\n\n\ndef _row_to_book(row):\n    return {\n        \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n        \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n        \"author\": row[\"author\"],\n        \"year\": row[\"year\"],\n        \"isbn\": row[\"isbn\"],\n    }\n\n\nclass Database:\n    \"\"\"Small thread-safe wrapper around a SQLite connection for books.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, path=\"books.db\"):\n        self._lock = threading.Lock()\n        self._conn = sqlite3.connect(path, check_same_thread=False)\n        self._conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        with self._lock:\n            self._conn.executescript(SCHEMA)\n            self._conn.commit()\n\n    def close(self):\n        with self._lock:\n            self._conn.close()\n\n    def ping(self):\n        with self._lock:\n            self._conn.execute(\"SELECT 1\").fetchone()\n\n    def list_books(self, author=None):\n        query = _SELECT\n        params = []\n        if author:\n            query += \" WHERE instr(lower(author), lower(?)) > 0\"\n            params.append(author)\n        query += \" ORDER BY id\"\n        with self._lock:\n            rows = self._conn.execute(query, params).fetchall()\n        return [_row_to_book(row) for row in rows]\n\n    def get_book(self, book_id):\n        with self._lock:\n            row = self._conn.execute(\n                _SELECT + \" WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)\n            ).fetchone()\n        return _row_to_book(row) if row is not None else None\n\n    def create_book(self, title, author, year=None, isbn=None):\n        with self._lock:\n            cursor = self._conn.execute(\n                \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n                (title, author, year, isbn),\n            )\n            self._conn.commit()\n            book_id = cursor.lastrowid\n        return self.get_book(book_id)\n\n    def update_book(self, book_id, fields):\n        allowed = {\n            name: fields[name]\n            for name in (\"title\", \"author\", \"year\", \"isbn\")\n            if name in fields\n        }\n        if allowed:\n            assignments = \", \".join(f\"{name} = ?\" for name in allowed)\n            params = [*allowed.values(), book_id]\n            with self._lock:\n                self._conn.execute(\n                    f\"UPDATE books SET {assignments} WHERE id = ?\", params\n                )\n                self._conn.commit()\n        return self.get_book(book_id)\n\n    def delete_book(self, book_id):\n        with self._lock:\n            cursor = self._conn.execute(\n                \"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)\n            )\n            self._conn.commit()\n            return cursor.rowcount > 0\n```\n\nHmm wait, there's a docstring convention question — CLAUDE.md mentions a documentation-style skill with Why/What/Test conventions... The system prompt says to follow code conventions; the available documentation-style skill is for documentation artifacts. I won't load the skill; I'll write reasonable docstrings without over-formatting. Actually the instructions say \"Load a specialized skill when the task matches one of the skills listed\" — documentation-style says \"Use when writing or reviewing any documentation artifact.\" Writing README.md could match... The skills are optional guidance; loading documentation-style might impose SLD conventions. Given constraints (concise output), I'll write a clean conventional README without loading the skill. Hmm — but the instruction \"Use the skill tool to load a skill when the task matches one of the skills\" is fairly directive. The task: implement REST API + README + tests. Candidate skills: api-design-patterns? documentation-style? test-driven-development? code-production-process? Loading many skills adds tokens. The core ask is straightforward implementation. I'll skip skill loading — the task is well-specified and my approach is standard. (Also \"IMPORTANT: minimize output tokens\".) Reasonable judgment: proceed without.\n\n**app.py** — final:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API.\n\nFlask application exposing CRUD endpoints for books backed by SQLite.\nRun with ``python app.py`` or ``flask --app app run``.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os\n\nfrom flask import Flask, jsonify, request\nfrom werkzeug.exceptions import HTTPException\n\nfrom db import Database\n\nBOOK_FIELDS = (\"title\", \"author\", \"year\", \"isbn\")\n\n\ndef create_app(config=None):\n    \"\"\"Application factory.\n\n    ``config`` overrides defaults; ``DATABASE`` selects the SQLite file\n    (or ``:memory:``). The ``BOOKS_DB`` environment variable provides the\n    default database path.\n    \"\"\"\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config.setdefault(\"DATABASE\", os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\"))\n    if config:\n        app.config.update(config)\n\n    db = Database(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n    app.extensions[\"books_db\"] = db\n\n    @app.get(\"/health\")\n    def health():\n        try:\n            db.ping()\n        except sqlite3.Error → wait, need import sqlite3? Just Exception.\n```\n\nHmm — catching bare `Exception` for ping. Import sqlite3 in app.py just for the error type? `except sqlite3.Error` is more precise. I'll import sqlite3. Fine.\n\nLet me write out app.py fully:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API.\n\nA Flask service exposing CRUD endpoints for a book collection stored in\nSQLite. Run with ``python app.py`` (see README.md for details).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport os\nimport sqlite3\n\nfrom flask import Flask, jsonify, request\nfrom werkzeug.exceptions import HTTPException\n\nfrom db import Database\n\nBOOK_FIELDS = (\"title\", \"author\", \"year\", \"isbn\")\n\n\ndef create_app(config=None):\n    \"\"\"Create and configure the Flask application.\n\n    ``config`` is an optional dict merged over the defaults. The database\n    location defaults to the ``BOOKS_DB`` environment variable, falling\n    back to ``books.db`` in the working directory; ``:memory:`` keeps the\n    database in RAM for tests.\n    \"\"\"\n    app = Flask(__name__)\n    app.config.setdefault(\"DATABASE\", os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\"))\n    if config:\n        app.config.update(config)\n\n    db = Database(app.config[\"DATABASE\"])\n    app.extensions[\"books_db\"] = db\n\n    @app.get(\"/health\")\n    def health():\n        try:\n            db.ping()\n        except sqlite3.Error:\n            return jsonify({\"status\": \"error\"}), 503\n        return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n    @app.get(\"/books\")\n    def list_books():\n        author = request.args.get(\"author\", \"\").strip()\n        return jsonify(db.list_books(author=author or None))\n\n    @app.post(\"/books\")\n    def create_book():\n        data, error = _json_object_or_error()\n        if error is not None:\n            return error\n        errors = validate_book_payload(data, partial=False)\n        if errors:\n            return _validation_error(errors)\n        book = db.create_book(\n            title=data[\"title\"],\n            author=data[\"author\"],\n            year=data.get(\"year\"),\n            isbn=data.get(\"isbn\"),\n        )\n        return jsonify(book), 201\n\n    @app.get(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\n    def get_book(book_id):\n        book = db.get_book(book_id)\n        if book is None:\n            return _not_found()\n        return jsonify(book)\n\n    @app.put(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\n    def update_book(book_id):\n        data, error = _json_object_or_error()\n        if error is not None:\n            return error\n        errors = validate_book_payload(data, partial=True)\n        if errors:\n            return _validation_error(errors)\n        fields = {name: data[name] for name in BOOK_FIELDS if name in data}\n        book = db.update_book(book_id, fields)\n        if book is None:\n            return _not_found()\n        return jsonify(book)\n\n    @app.delete(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")\n    def delete_book(book_id):\n        if not db.delete_book(book_id):\n            return _not_found()\n        return \"\", 204\n\n    @app.errorhandler(HTTPException)\n    def handle_http_exception(exc):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": exc.description or exc.name}), exc.code or 500\n\n    @app.errorhandler(Exception)\n    def handle_unexpected_error(exc):\n        return jsonify({\"error\": \"Internal server error\"}), 500\n\n    return app\n\n\ndef validate_book_payload(data, partial):\n    \"\"\"Validate a book payload.\n\n    ``title`` and ``author`` are required unless ``partial`` is true (used\n    by PUT, where only supplied fields are validated). Returns a dict of\n    field name -> error message; empty dict means the payload is valid.\n    \"\"\"\n    errors = {}\n    for field in (\"title\", \"author\"):\n        if field in data:\n            value = data[field]\n            if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n                errors[field] = f\"{field} must be a non-empty string\"\n        elif not partial:\n            errors[field] = f\"{field} is required\"\n    if \"year\" in data and data[\"year\"] is not None:\n        year = data[\"year\"]\n        if isinstance(year, bool) or not isinstance(year, int):\n            errors[\"year\"] = \"year must be an integer\"\n    if \"isbn\" in data and data[\"isbn\"] is not None:\n        if not isinstance(data[\"isbn\"], str):\n            errors[\"isbn\"] = \"isbn must be a string\"\n    return errors\n\n\ndef _json_object_or_error():\n    \"\"\"Parse the request body as a JSON object.\n\n    Returns ``(data, None)`` on success or ``(None, (response, status))``\n    when the body is missing, not valid JSON, or not an object.\n    \"\"\"\n    data = request.get_json(force=True, silent=True)\n    if data is None:\n        return None, (jsonify({\"error\": \"Request body must be valid JSON\"}), 400)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        return None, (jsonify({\"error\": \"Request body must be a JSON object\"}), 400)\n    return data, None\n\n\ndef _validation_error(details):\n    return jsonify({\"error\": \"Validation failed\", \"details\": details}), 400\n\n\ndef _not_found():\n    return jsonify({\"error\": \"Book not found\"}), 404\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    application = create_app()\n    application.run(\n        host=os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"127.0.0.1\"),\n        port=int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"5000\")),\n    )\n```\n\nWait — one thing: `_json_object_or_error` returns tuple `(response, status)` — in the route I `return error` where error is that tuple → Flask accepts (body, status) tuple. ✓\n\nAlso — `handle_http_exception` when exc.code is None? Some HTTPExceptions... code always set for real ones; `or 500` guards.\n\nHmm, one more: the generic `Exception` handler — when Flask is in TESTING mode with test_client, propagate_exceptions defaults to True → handler bypassed, exceptions raised in tests. Fine.\n\nAlso — Werkzeug may raise `BadRequest` from get_json if not silent — I use silent=True, so no.\n\n**The `_json_object_or_error` with force=True**: note — force=True ignores content-type; body \"not json\" with content-type application/json → parse error → None → 400 ✓. Empty body → None → 400 ✓. Body `\"null\"` → parses to None → 400 \"must be valid JSON\" (message slightly off for explicit null, but fine). Body `\"[1,2]\"` → list → 400 \"must be a JSON object\" ✓. Body `\"42\"` → int → not dict → 400 ✓.\n\n**tests/conftest.py:**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Shared pytest fixtures for the book API tests.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom app import create_app\n\n\n@pytest.fixture()\ndef client():\n    \"\"\"A Flask test client backed by a fresh in-memory database.\"\"\"\n    app = create_app({\"TESTING\": True, \"DATABASE\": \":memory:\"})\n    with app.test_client() as test_client:\n        yield test_client\n    app.extensions[\"books_db\"].close()\n```\n\n**tests/test_books.py:**\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Integration tests for the book collection REST API.\"\"\"\n\nBOOK = {\n    \"title\": \"Dune\",\n    \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\",\n    \"year\": 1965,\n    \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\",\n}\n\n\ndef create_book(client, **overrides):\n    payload = dict(BOOK)\n    payload.update(overrides)\n    return client.post(\"/books\", json=payload)\n\n\nclass TestHealth:\n    def test_returns_ok_status(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/health\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n\n\nclass TestCreateBook:\n    def test_creates_book_and_returns_201(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client)\n        assert response.status_code == 201\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"id\": 1, **BOOK}\n\n    def test_year_and_isbn_are_optional(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\n            \"/books\", json={\"title\": \"The Hobbit\", \"author\": \"J. R. R. Tolkien\"}\n        )\n        assert response.status_code == 201\n        body = response.get_json()\n        assert body[\"title\"] == \"The Hobbit\"\n        assert body[\"year\"] is None\n        assert body[\"isbn\"] is None\n\n    def test_missing_title_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"author\": \"Frank Herbert\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        body = response.get_json()\n        assert body == {\n            \"error\": \"Validation failed\",\n            \"details\": {\"title\": \"title is required\"},\n        }\n\n    def test_missing_author_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Dune\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"author\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_blank_title_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client, title=\"   \")\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"title\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_non_string_title_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client, title=42)\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"title\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_non_integer_year_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client, year=\"nineteen eighty-four\")\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"year\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_boolean_year_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client, year=True)\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"year\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_non_string_isbn_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = create_book(client, isbn=12345)\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"isbn\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_invalid_json_body_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\n            \"/books\", data=\"not json\", content_type=\"application/json\"\n        )\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"error\" in response.get_json()\n\n    def test_non_object_json_body_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\n            \"/books\", data=\"[1, 2, 3]\", content_type=\"application/json\"\n        )\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"error\" in response.get_json()\n\n    def test_missing_body_is_rejected(self, client):\n        response = client.post(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n\nclass TestListBooks:\n    def test_empty_collection_returns_empty_list(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == []\n\n    def test_lists_all_books_in_creation_order(self, client):\n        create_book(client)\n        create_book(client, title=\"1984\", author=\"George Orwell\", year=1949)\n        response = client.get(\"/books\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        books = response.get_json()\n        assert [book[\"id\"] for book in books] == [1, 2]\n        assert [book[\"title\"] for book in books] == [\"Dune\", \"1984\"]\n\n    def test_author_filter_matches_case_insensitively(self, client):\n        create_book(client)\n        create_book(client, title=\"1984\", author=\"George Orwell\", year=1949)\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"frank herbert\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        books = response.get_json()\n        assert len(books) == 1\n        assert books[0][\"author\"] == \"Frank Herbert\"\n\n    def test_author_filter_matches_partial_names(self, client):\n        create_book(client)\n        create_book(client, title=\"1984\", author=\"George Orwell\", year=1949)\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"orwell\"})\n        assert [b[\"author\"] for b in response.get_json()] == [\"George Orwell\"]\n\n    def test_author_filter_with_no_match_returns_empty_list(self, client):\n        create_book(client)\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"Nobody\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == []\n\n    def test_empty_author_param_returns_all_books(self, client):\n        create_book(client)\n        response = client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert len(response.get_json()) == 1\n\n\nclass TestGetBook:\n    def test_returns_book_by_id(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == created\n\n    def test_missing_book_returns_404(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books/999\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"error\": \"Book not found\"}\n\n    def test_non_integer_id_returns_404(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/books/abc\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert \"error\" in response.get_json()\n\n\nclass TestUpdateBook:\n    def test_updates_single_field(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"year\": 1966})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        body = response.get_json()\n        assert body[\"year\"] == 1966\n        assert body[\"title\"] == created[\"title\"]\n        assert body[\"author\"] == created[\"author\"]\n\n    def test_updates_all_fields(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        payload = {\n            \"title\": \"Dune Messiah\",\n            \"author\": \"F. Herbert\",\n            \"year\": 1969,\n            \"isbn\": \"978-0441172696\",\n        }\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json=payload)\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"id\": created[\"id\"], **payload}\n\n    def test_missing_book_returns_404(self, client):\n        response = client.put(\"/books/999\", json={\"title\": \"Nope\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"error\": \"Book not found\"}\n\n    def test_blank_title_is_rejected(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"title\": \"  \"})\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"title\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_invalid_year_is_rejected(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"year\": \"x\"})\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n        assert \"year\" in response.get_json()[\"details\"]\n\n    def test_empty_object_leaves_book_unchanged(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={})\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == created\n\n    def test_invalid_json_body_is_rejected(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(\n            f\"/books/{created['id']}\", data=\"not json\", content_type=\"application/json\"\n        )\n        assert response.status_code == 400\n\n    def test_unknown_fields_are_ignored(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.put(\n            f\"/books/{created['id']}\", json={\"publisher\": \"Chilton Books\"}\n        )\n        assert response.status_code == 200\n        assert response.get_json() == created\n\n\nclass TestDeleteBook:\n    def test_deletes_book_and_returns_204(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        response = client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.status_code == 204\n        assert response.get_data() == b\"\"\n        assert client.get(f\"/books/{created['id']}\").status_code == 404\n\n    def test_missing_book_returns_404(self, client):\n        response = client.delete(\"/books/999\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.get_json() == {\"error\": \"Book not found\"}\n\n    def test_deleting_twice_returns_404(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        response = client.delete(f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n\n\nclass TestApiContract:\n    def test_unknown_route_returns_json_404(self, client):\n        response = client.get(\"/nope\")\n        assert response.status_code == 404\n        assert response.content_type.startswith(\"application/json\")\n\n    def test_unsupported_method_returns_json_405(self, client):\n        response = client.patch(\"/books/1\")\n        assert response.status_code == 405\n        assert response.content_type.startswith(\"application/json\")\n\n    def test_responses_use_json_content_type(self, client):\n        created = create_book(client).get_json()\n        for method, path in [\n            (\"GET\", \"/health\"),\n            (\"GET\", \"/books\"),\n            (\"GET\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\"),\n        ]:\n            response = client.open(path, method=method)\n            assert response.content_type.startswith(\"application/json\")\n```\n\nPlus unit tests for Database:\n\n```python\nclass TestDatabaseLayer:\n    def test_crud_roundtrip(self):\n        from db import Database\n\n        db = Database(\":memory:\")\n        try:\n            book = db.create_book(\"Dune\", \"Frank Herbert\", 1965, \"978-0441172719\")\n            assert book == {\n                \"id\": 1,\n                \"title\": \"Dune\",\n                \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\",\n                \"year\": 1965,\n                \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\",\n            }\n            assert db.get_book(1) == book\n            assert db.list_books() == [book]\n\n            updated = db.update_book(1, {\"year\": 1966})\n            assert updated[\"year\"] == 1966\n\n            assert db.delete_book(1) is True\n            assert db.get_book(1) is None\n            assert db.list_books() == []\n        finally:\n            db.close()\n\n    def test_update_missing_book_returns_none(self):\n        from db import Database\n\n        db = Database(\":memory:\")\n        try:\n            assert db.update_book(42, {\"title\": \"Ghost\"}) is None\n        finally:\n            db.close()\n\n    def test_delete_missing_book_returns_false(self):\n        from db import Database\n\n        db = Database(\":memory:\")\n        try:\n            assert db.delete_book(42) is False\n        finally:\n            db.close()\n```\n\nMove imports to top of file instead of inline. Yes — `from db import Database` at top.\n\nCount: 1 health + 12 create + 6 list + 3 get + 8 update + 3 delete + 3 contract + 3 db = 39 tests. Plenty ≥ 3. \n\nHmm wait — one test to double check: `test_creates_book_and_returns_201` asserts `response.get_json() == {\"id\": 1, **BOOK}` — dict equality ignores order ✓.\n\n`test_updates_all_fields` asserts `{\"id\": created[\"id\"], **payload}` ✓.\n\nIn `test_author_filter_matches_partial_names` — \"orwell\" in \"george orwell\" (lowered) ✓.\n\nOne thing to verify: `client.get(\"/books\", query_string={\"author\": \"frank herbert\"})` — Werkzeug encodes space properly ✓.\n\n`test_missing_body_is_rejected`: `client.post(\"/books\")` — no content type, no data → get_json(force=True, silent=True) → None → 400 ✓.\n\nNow **README.md**:\n\n```markdown\n# Book Collection REST API\n\nA small REST API for managing a book collection, written in Python with\n[Flask](https://flask.palletsprojects.com/) and\n[SQLite](https://www.sqlite.org/). Books are persisted in a local SQLite\ndatabase file.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Python 3.10+\n- pip\n\n## Setup\n\n```bash\npython -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate      # Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n## Running the server\n\n```bash\npython app.py\n```\n\nThe service listens on `http://127.0.0.1:5000` by default.\n\nConfiguration (environment variables):\n\n| Variable  | Default    | Description                          |\n|-----------|------------|--------------------------------------|\n| `BOOKS_DB`| `books.db` | Path of the SQLite database file     |\n| `HOST`    | `127.0.0.1`| Bind address                          |\n| `PORT`    | `5000`     | Listen port                           |\n\nAlternatively, use the Flask CLI (supports `--debug` for auto-reload):\n\n```bash\nflask --app app run --debug\n```\n\n## API\n\nAll request and response bodies are JSON. A book looks like this:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"title\": \"Dune\",\n  \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\",\n  \"year\": 1965,\n  \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"\n}\n```\n\n`id` is assigned by the server. `year` and `isbn` are optional and may be\n`null`; `title` and `author` are required and must be non-empty strings.\n\n### `GET /health`\n\nHealth check. Verifies the database is reachable.\n\n- `200` — `{\"status\": \"ok\"}`\n- `503` — `{\"status\": \"error\"}` if the database is unreachable\n\n### `POST /books`\n\nCreates a book. ...\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5000/books \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"}'\n```\n\n- `201` — the created book (with its `id`)\n- `400` — validation error...\n\nValidation rules:\n- `title` — required, non-empty string\n- `author` — required, non-empty string\n- `year` — optional, integer\n- `isbn` — optional, string\n- unknown fields are ignored\n\n### `GET /books`\n\nLists all books, ordered by `id`. Optional `?author=` filter performs a\ncase-insensitive substring match on the author name.\n\n```bash\ncurl http://127.0.0.1:5000/books\ncurl \"http://127.0.0.1:5000/books?author=orwell\"\n```\n\n- `200` — JSON array of books (empty array when none match)\n\n### `GET /books/{id}`\n\n- `200` — the book\n- `404` — `{\"error\": \"Book not found\"}`\n\n### `PUT /books/{id}`\n\nUpdates a book. Any subset of the writable fields (`title`, `author`,\n`year`, `isbn`) may be sent; only the supplied fields are changed.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X PUT http://127.0.0.1:5000/books/1 \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"year\": 1966}'\n```\n\n- `200` — the updated book\n- `400` — validation error (same rules as POST, applied to supplied fields)\n- `404` — book not found\n\n### `DELETE /books/{id}`\n\n```bash\ncurl -X DELETE http://127.0.0.1:5000/books/1\n```\n\n- `204` — deleted (empty body)\n- `404` — book not found\n\n### Errors\n\nValidation failures return `400`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"error\": \"Validation failed\",\n  \"details\": {\n    \"title\": \"title is required\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nMalformed request bodies return `400` with `{\"error\": \"Request body must be\nvalid JSON\"}`. Unknown URLs and unsupported methods return JSON `404`/`405`\nresponses.\n\n## Running the tests\n\n```bash\npytest\n```\n\nThe suite contains unit tests for the database layer and integration tests\nfor every endpoint (create, list, filter, get, update, delete, validation,\nand error paths). Tests run against a temporary in-memory database and do\nnot touch `books.db`.\n\n## Project layout\n\n```\napp.py             Flask application: routes, validation, error handling\ndb.py              SQLite data-access layer\ntests/             pytest test suite\nrequirements.txt   Runtime + test dependencies\npytest.ini         pytest configuration\n```\n```\n\n**requirements.txt:**\n```\nflask>=3.0\npytest>=8.0\n```\n\n**pytest.ini:**\n```ini\n[pytest]\ntestpaths = tests\npythonpath = .\n```\n\n**.gitignore:**\n```\n__pycache__/\n*.pyc\n.pytest_cache/\nvenv/\nbooks.db\n```\n\nNow let me create the todo list and start writing files. Actually — is a todo list worth it here? Multi-step: write 8 files, run tests, smoke test. Sure, brief todo list helps track. The guidance suggests using todowrite for multi-step work. I'll create a small one.\n\nThen verification:\n1. compileall\n2. pytest -v\n3. smoke test script\n\nAlso — the CLAUDE.md says prefer trusty-search for code search, but this is a fresh empty workspace; no index needed. Skip.\n\nOne more consideration — `_agent_stdout.log` etc: leave.\n\nLet me also double check the `handle_http_exception` description for 405: werkzeug's MethodNotAllowed description mentions valid methods... it's fine.\n\nPotential issue: Flask's `@app.errorhandler(HTTPException)` — does registering for the base class work? Yes, Flask supports registering handlers for base classes of exceptions; it walks the MRO. ✓\n\nOne more: in `test_unsupported_method_returns_json_405` — PATCH /books/1: werkzeug raises 405 with Allow header; handler returns JSON ✓.\n\nWhat about HEAD /books → automatic HEAD support (Flask adds HEAD/GET). Fine.\n\nAlso OPTIONS → Flask auto-options. Fine.\n\nNow — smoke script. Let me write it to the temp dir `/var/folders/t6/p1v727892_qc9xqqdzq2rpym0000gn/T/opencode/smoke.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -u\ncd \"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-d4c726zc/retort-0d01ce548f6e\"\nDB=/tmp/books_smoke.db\nrm -f \"$DB\"\nBOOKS_DB=\"$DB\" HOST=127.0.0.1 PORT=5099 venv/bin/python app.py >/tmp/books_smoke_server.log 2>&1 &\nSERVER_PID=$!\ntrap 'kill $SERVER_PID 2>/dev/null; rm -f \"$DB\"' EXIT\nfor i in $(seq 1 40); do\n  curl -s -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:5099/health && break\n  sleep 0.25\ndone\n\nreq() { # method path [data]\n  local method=$1 path=$2 data=${3:-}\n  echo \"== $method $path\"\n  if [ -n \"$data\" ]; then\n    curl -s -w \"\\n-> %{http_code}\\n\" -X \"$method\" \"http://127.0.0.1:5099$path\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d \"$data\"\n  else\n    curl -s -w \"\\n-> %{http_code}\\n\" -X \"$method\" \"http://127.0.0.1:5099$path\"\n  fi\n}\n\nreq GET /health\nreq POST /books '{\"title\":\"Dune\",\"author\":\"Frank Herbert\",\"year\":1965,\"isbn\":\"978-0441172719\"}'\nreq POST /books '{\"title\":\"1984\",\"author\":\"George Orwell\",\"year\":1949}'\nreq POST /books '{\"year\":2000}'\nreq POST /books 'not json'\nreq GET /books\nreq GET \"/books?author=orwell\"\nreq GET /books/1\nreq GET /books/999\nreq GET /books/abc\nreq PUT /books/1 '{\"year\":1966}'\nreq PUT /books/999 '{\"year\":1966}'\nreq DELETE /books/2\nreq DELETE /books/2\nreq PATCH /books/1 '{}'\necho \"SMOKE DONE\"\n```\n\nWait — the health-wait loop: `curl -s -o /dev/null URL && break` — if server not up, curl fails (exit non-zero) → loop continues. ✓ \n\nNote the trap kills server on exit. Also final `kill` after script (trap handles). The server runs Flask dev server (not debug) — fine in background.\n\nThen run `bash /var/folders/.../opencode/smoke.sh`.\n\nAlso — check the server log for errors after smoke: `cat /tmp/books_smoke_server.log` — use Read tool? It's in /tmp — Read tool can read any absolute path. Or just leave it; only check if something fails. I'll check quickly via bash tail? Guidance says avoid cat... use Read tool on the log after. Eh, only if needed.\n\nExpected smoke results:\n- GET /health → 200 {\"status\":\"ok\"}\n- POST 1 → 201 book id 1\n- POST 2 → 201 id 2\n- POST {\"year\":2000} → 400 validation\n- POST not json → 400\n- GET /books → 2 books\n- ?author=orwell → 1 book (1984)\n- GET /books/1 → 200\n- GET /books/999 → 404\n- GET /books/abc → 404\n- PUT /books/1 year 1966 → 200\n- PUT /books/999 → 404\n- DELETE /books/2 → 204 (empty body — curl -w shows just the code line)\n- DELETE /books/2 again → 404\n- PATCH /books/1 → 405\n\nOne caveat — `req DELETE /books/2`: my req function with data empty → curl -X DELETE without body ✓.\n\nAlso curl on macOS: `curl -s -w \"\\n-> %{http_code}\\n\"` ✓.\n\nAlright — also decide whether to run tests with coverage? pytest-cov installed. Not required. Plain pytest -v is fine. Maybe show concise output.\n\nAlso — should I run `venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt` to verify requirements file validity? Quick, harmless: it'll say already satisfied. Sure, do it as part of verification.\n\nAlso compileall: `venv/bin/python -m compileall -q app.py db.py tests` then `echo ok`.\n\nLet me also double-check one potential test failure: `test_responses_use_json_content_type` uses `client.open(path, method=method)` — fine.\n\nAnd in TestApiContract PATCH — hmm wait, does `client.patch` exist? Yes, Flask test client has .patch. ✓\n\nAnother potential gotcha: `response.get_data() == b\"\"` for 204 — Flask returns empty body ✓.\n\nNow, one FINAL design double-check on PUT validation: `{\"title\": null}` in PUT → title in data, value None → not isinstance str → error \"title must be a non-empty string\" → 400. Good — can't clear required fields via null. ✓ And POST with title null → same. ✓\n\nAnd POST `{\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Herbert\", \"year\": null}` → year None allowed → stored NULL → 201. ✓ (year null means \"no year\").\n\nWhat about extra whitespace title \" Dune \" → strip() non-empty → valid → stored with spaces. OK.\n\nEdge: title with only newline \"\\n\" → strip → \"\" → 400 ✓.\n\nI think the design is solid. Let me also reconsider: **jsonify sort_keys** — Flask 3 default `sort_keys=True`. Response key order alphabetical — no test depends on order. ✓\n\nOne more: **Flask 3.1 `get_json(force=True, silent=True)`** — signature still accepts force & silent ✓.\n\n**sqlite3 autocommit behavior**: default isolation_level=\"\" (deferred) → implicit transactions; I commit after each write explicitly. Reads without commit fine. executescript auto-commits pending then runs script. ✓ \n\n**lastrowid** after commit — captured before? I read `cursor.lastrowid` after commit; lastrowid remains valid on the cursor. ✓ (It's set after execute, persists.)\n\nAlright, writing files now. Order: db.py, app.py, tests/conftest.py, tests/test_books.py, requirements.txt, pytest.ini, .gitignore, README.md, smoke script; then run verification.\n\nAlso — the todowrite. Let me define:\n1. Write application code (app.py, db.py) — in_progress\n2. Write test suite (tests/)\n3. Write README + config files\n4. Run tests & smoke-test the running server\n\nLet me go. I'll write files with the write tool.\n\nWait — one thing about writing `README.md`: instructions say \"NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files. Only create documentation files if explicitly requested\" — here TASK.md explicitly requests README.md as a deliverable. ✓ Explicitly requested.\n\nAlso the user said \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — tests/ subdir is fine (source tree convention), still within the workspace. \"current directory\" = workspace root. ✓\n\nLet me write everything now.\n\nOne more paranoid check on pytest.ini `pythonpath = .` — the `.` is relative to rootdir (where pytest.ini lives). ✓ And `testpaths = tests`. If someone runs `pytest tests/test_books.py::TestHealth` explicitly, testpaths ignored, pythonpath still applies. ✓\n\nAlso — should tests/__init__.py exist to avoid name collision issues? If another `test_books.py` existed elsewhere... no. Skip.\n\nWriting now.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the \"id\": 1 assertion once more — fixture creates a fresh app + fresh :memory: DB per test (fixture is function-scoped by default) → AUTOINCREMENT starts at 1. ✓ Deterministic.\n\nAnd in `test_lists_all_books_in_creation_order` — ids [1,2] ✓.\n\nOK writing files.\n\ndef create_book in test helper — shadows view function name in app module? No conflict (different modules).\n\nAlso, minor: in tests/conftest.py, after `with app.test_client() as test_client: yield test_client` — teardown after context exit: `app.extensions[\"books_db\"].close()` ✓.\n\nFinal file contents settled. Writing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_058e26c70001v3Pwn1mxAl96AB","sessionID":"ses_fa724aa9dffedl8WPGA1qUutze","messageID":"msg_058dbf8c1001QkitxIw3kF4T1x"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788197629060,"sessionID":"ses_fa724aa9dffedl8WPGA1qUutze","part":{"id":"prt_058e261d5001etY9x4A7VrooOT","messageID":"msg_058dbf8c1001QkitxIw3kF4T1x","sessionID":"ses_fa724aa9dffedl8WPGA1qUutze","type":"text","text":"I'll read the task and set up a plan. TASK.md asks for a Python REST API for books (CRUD + health check, SQLite, validation, README, ≥3 tests). The stack file says framework is my choice — I'll use Flask (already installable in the provided venv).","time":{"start":1788197626326,"end":1788197629057}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788197629234,"sessionID":"ses_fa724aa9dffedl8WPGA1qUutze","part":{"id":"prt_058e26d30001tpog4m5DOedfCc","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"bd9e98d0c0a23cf09481524dce6da963bf6e429c","messageID":"msg_058dbf8c1001QkitxIw3kF4T1x","sessionID":"ses_fa724aa9dffedl8WPGA1qUutze","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":100464,"input":377,"output":193,"reasoning":28022,"cache":{"write":0,"read":71872}},"cost":0.14336052}}
